  "assessment": "All three layouts are closely grouped within each dataset/beta setting. For MNIST beta1, F4/FH64 has the lowest recorded negative ELBO (90.5943); F4/FH32 and F2/FH64 are close at 90.7509 and 90.8676. For Fashion-MNIST beta1, F4/FH64 is lowest at 232.7821, but F4/FH32 (232.8148) and F2/FH64 (232.9599) are effectively close in this single-seed comparison. For Fashion-MNIST beta4, F2/FH64 (243.8159) and F4/FH32 (243.8420) are nearly tied, with F4/FH64 at 244.0994. Beta1 gives better ordinary ELBO and reconstruction MSE than beta4 in this common Bernoulli protocol. Across the inspected prior grids at seeds 42, 123 and 2026, the three architectures have broadly similar recognizable shapes within a beta setting; no clear general generation winner is established by conditioner width or coupling count. On MNIST, beta4 generally makes digit shapes softer and simpler, while beta1 retains sharper and more varied stroke styles, including some ambiguous shapes. The earlier preference for beta4 concerned coherence rather than an objective or universal improvement. On Fashion-MNIST, beta1 retains more visible variation and structure; beta4 gives smoother, more generic clothing silhouettes. Fine textures remain weak in both. Changing beta has a larger observed visual and metric effect than the small flow layout changes. F2 is a reasonable economical candidate, with no compelling visible disadvantage in these grids; the small metric differences should not be treated as statistically established rankings. The three inference seeds are not independent training repeats. No new training beyond the eight missing configurations was performed; one requested configuration and three contextual MNIST beta4 models were reused.",
